assistant_response_en:
I can’t verify **real-time** earthquake emergencies from here. As of my last reliable general knowledge, the well-known major Kermanshah earthquake emergency was the **2017 Iran–Iraq border earthquake**, with later aftershocks—not an ongoing “major emergency.” To confirm whether there is a **current** major earthquake emergency in Kermanshah, check official/current sources such as:

- **USGS earthquake map**: https://earthquake.usgs.gov/earthquakes/map  
- **EMSC**: https://www.emsc-csem.org  
- **GDACS disaster alerts**: https://www.gdacs.org  
- **ReliefWeb** Iran updates: https://reliefweb.int/country/irn  
- Iranian Red Crescent / reputable news agencies

If your caseworker says there is an active emergency, ask them for the source or official alert.

On the Germany asylum question: an earthquake in your home region is **not normally, by itself, a legal basis for being granted asylum**, because asylum/refugee protection is mainly about persecution, war/serious harm, etc., not natural disasters. However, you can usually **request faster processing or an earlier hearing** from BAMF on humanitarian/urgent grounds. Whether BAMF agrees is discretionary and depends on the circumstances.

A current disaster might support an expedite request if you can explain why it is directly urgent for your case, for example:

- your close family is missing/injured or you need urgent contact/documentation;
- the emergency affects evidence or witnesses relevant to your asylum claim;
- it creates severe psychological hardship supported by medical/psychological evidence;
- your personal risk situation has changed in a way relevant to protection.

But “there is an earthquake in my province” alone may not be enough.

You can ask your lawyer or adviser to submit a written request to BAMF for an earlier hearing, including:

- your BAMF file number/Aktenzeichen;
- proof of the current earthquake emergency from reliable sources;
- proof your family is in the affected area;
- a short explanation of why this makes your asylum case urgent;
- any medical/psychological documentation if relevant.

Also, clarify whether your “caseworker” means a **social worker/adviser** or an official from **BAMF**—because only BAMF/courts can actually move asylum procedure dates.
